WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
<v Instructor>Now let's talk about Amazon OpenSearch,</v>

2
00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:05.370
which is the new name of ElasticSearch service.

3
00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.870
So it's very important to understand

4
00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.210
that ElasticSearch in the exam should now

5
00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.904
become OpenSearch.

6
00:00:10.904 --> 00:00:12.630
And Kibana used what used

7
00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.810
to be Kibana is now OpenSearch Dashboards.

8
00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.147
So it's a managed version of OpenSearch,

9
00:00:18.147 --> 00:00:20.070
the Amazon OpenSearch service,

10
00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.720
which is an open source project,

11
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.400
and it's a fork of ElasticSearch.

12
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.430
So ElasticSearch has changed its type of license,

13
00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.602
and AWS had to do their own open source project

14
00:00:29.602 --> 00:00:33.330
to allow the project to be hosted on Amazon,

15
00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:34.800
hence the new name.

16
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.240
So you have two modes of operation.

17
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.040
The first one is Manage Cluster,

18
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.100
where you actually define the types of servers you want,

19
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.720
or you can use a serverless cluster

20
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.480
to just simply use Amazon OpenSearch.

21
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.790
And the use cases are going to be log analytics,

22
00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:51.150
real time application monitoring, security analytics,

23
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:55.530
full text search, clickstream analytics, and indexing.

24
00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.260
So you can use the full suite of OpenSearch,

25
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.900
OpenSearch, Dashboards, Logstash.

26
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.310
So OpenSearch provides you search and indexing capability.

27
00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.010
OpenSearch Dashboards, which used to be named Kibana,

28
00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.470
provides you realtime dashboards on top of the data

29
00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.150
that sits in OpenSearch.

30
00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.800
And it's going to be an alternative

31
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.120
to using CloudWatch Dashboards,

32
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.220
which gives you more advanced capabilities

33
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.420
for dashboarding

34
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.420
and Logstash is a log ingestion mechanism.

35
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.160
For this, you need to use the logs dash agent,

36
00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.820
and it's going to be an alternative to using CloudWatch Logs

37
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.223
where you decide on retention and granularity.

38
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.860
So here is an architecture sample

39
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.870
by using DynamoDB and OpenSearch.

40
00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:36.060
So we want to search on top of DynamoDB.

41
00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.460
So for this, all the Create updates

42
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.230
and delete in DynamoDB are going

43
00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.090
to end up into DynamoDB stream.

44
00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.410
We can create a Lambda function

45
00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.530
that will send all the data into Amazon OpenSearch.

46
00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.930
Please note that this flow can now be changed

47
00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.760
as you see in the previous lectures

48
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.300
from DynamoDB table to Kinesis data streams

49
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.600
to Canis data Firehose to Amazon OpenSearch

50
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:01.050
because now we have this capability from within DynamoDB

51
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:04.080
to send data into Kinesis data streams.

52
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.840
Anyway, once the data is in Amazon OpenSearch,

53
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.450
then you can create your own API, for example,

54
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.700
your own application to search items.

55
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.333
And this application can be hosted on say,

56
00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:17.280
an EC2 instance behind a load balancer.

57
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.740
And then once the items are being searched,

58
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.520
then they can be retrieved directly from DynamoDB table.

59
00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.930
So DynamoDB table we know is very good

60
00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:28.140
for very simple row level up operations,

61
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.550
but then OpenSearch is good for search.

62
00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.980
So the two together gives you an API to search items

63
00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.563
and then retrieve them from the DynamoDB table.

64
00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:37.590
Another pattern is using CloudWatch Logs.

65
00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.360
So we can send the logs from CloudWatch

66
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.010
into a subscription filter,

67
00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.956
into a Lambda function that is managed by AWS

68
00:02:42.956 --> 00:02:47.040
that will send data in real time to Amazon OpenSearch.

69
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.480
Another way to do it would be

70
00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.850
to have your CloudWatch Logs send in again

71
00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.540
into a subscription filter into Kinesis data Firehose,

72
00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.300
and this time in near real time into Amazon OpenSearch

73
00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.950
because, well, Kinesis data Firehose

74
00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.440
has some batching capability.

75
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.540
Okay, that's it for Amazon OpenSearch.

76
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.383
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

